    "narrative": "The Austin Waves enter today\u2019s slate with **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** and **Gunnar Henderson** headlining the lineup as the clear pillars of production. **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** earns an elite 84.88 score against Brandon Young, buoyed significantly by a +16% park factor and a +19% matchup adjustment against a starter with a 4.78 xERA. Similarly, **Gunnar Henderson** remains a high-floor play at 83.29, thriving in a park that inflates his raw projection despite facing the moderate challenge of Trey Yesavage. **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** also projects strongly at 70.88, as the optimizer heavily weights his favorable dynamic platoon advantage against Michael King, providing a reliable cornerstone at second base to chase R and RBI.\n\nStrategic benching remains a constant hurdle, with **Coby Mayo** emerging as the most difficult omission today. His score of 46.80 nearly nudged him into the utility or infield slots, but he fell just short due to a unfavorable batting order projection (#8) and a negative dynamic platoon adjustment, causing the optimizer to favor **Josh Jung**\u2019s 60.98 outing against the more hittable Seth Lugo. While **Kyle Tucker** carries a 52.36 rating, his performance is dampened by a -18% SP skill adjustment against Jes\u00fas Luzardo, reinforcing our focus on high-efficiency matchups. Managers should remain cautious regarding the \"Lineup Pending\" status for all starters; specifically, **Nasim Nunez** and **Andy Pages** face significant efficiency risks if they are slotted in the bottom third of the order, where their speed-based utility is minimized by fewer high-leverage plate appearances. \n\nBeyond the daily grind, we continue to monitor the status of **Jacob Wilson**, whose IL placement remains a primary concern for infield depth. While **Sam Antonacci** and **Brayan Rocchio** offer bench stability, their current scores under 45.00 confirm they are best kept in reserve to protect our game caps until a more distinct platoon advantage arises. We are also keeping a close eye on **Miguel Vargas** and **Miguel Andujar**; despite their moderate scores, both benefit from favorable stolen base environments or park factors that make them viable in standard 5x5 formats if the official lineups reflect the aggressive batting positions we have projected.",
